{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqg27k7p71f","workspace":"/Users/imyu/Desktop/zonoid","title":"Rerank blend sweep on current KB: α=0.5 K=50 is the sweet spot; widening the pool does not help recall here","summary":"Follow-up to note-mqg14209j5s. After replacing the hard [1,2] offset with the convex blend score=α·cosine+(1−α)·ce (ORCH_RERANK_ALPHA) and fixing the bench model-cache (symlink canonical models into the isolated daemon so the CE actually loads), re-ran the 28-query retrieval-bench against the live KB snapshot with warm models.\n\nRESULTS (OFF baseline → blend):\n- α=0.5, K=50: MRR@5 0.7036→0.7976 (+0.094), recall@3 0.75→0.8214 (+0.071), recall@5→0.8214, recall@10 0.8214→0.8214 (the hard-offset's recall@10 regression is FIXED). Sweet spot.\n- α=0.5, K=200 (rerank whole candidate set): recall flat 0.8214, MRR 0.7798 (LOWER than K=50). α=0.3 identical.\n\nKEY INSIGHT: on this small (~154-note) KB the recall ceiling (0.8214; recall@3==recall@10) is UPSTREAM of rerank — the missing ~18% of relevant notes are not retrieved at all (score ~0, never enter ragResults), so widening the rerank pool (raising K) cannot reach them. Widening K instead admitted more distractors the CE did not perfectly reject → small MRR drop. So \"relax the cosine floor / raise K\" is NOT a win at small scale; the blend at moderate K is. The floor-relaxation hypothesis is SCALE-DEPENDENT: a tight floor only excludes relevant-but-low-cosine notes when the corpus is large enough to have them — i.e. it must be tested at the 100x scale, not on the current KB. precision@k remains a weak metric here (single-relevant eval cases). Blend is committed (feature/rerank-tuning, ea93893); still DEFAULT OFF, still needs the 100x test before enabling.","created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-16T03:05:21.685Z","vec":[-0.04866636544466019,-0.07395748794078827,0.001992278965190053,0.030095815658569336,0.024282114580273628,0.05194676294922829,-0.025236371904611588,0.07798457890748978,0.01999557949602604,